  data:  # 83f8
    kind: work_item  # 83f8
    title: "Build the opacity-gated disclosure boundary for deferred crossings (ADR-0049 ruling 2) -- all three clauses or none; clause 3 is what stops it being a false-all-clear generator"  # 83f8
    status: todo_hard  # 83f8
    priority: 2  # 83f8
    parent:  # 83f8
    tags:  # 83f8
    - taint  # 83f8
    - catalogue  # 83f8
    - io_boundary  # 83f8
    isbefore: []  # 83f8
    duplicate_of: []  # 83f8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 83f8
    pr_ref:  # 83f8
    description: "FILED BY ADR-0049 §\"Open work\" steps 4-5. BLOCKED ON the census (WI-hazop) and the\nreachability pass (WI-vapud) -- ADR-0049 ruling 3 licenses no row move before both.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD. A catalog-declarable disclosure boundary (working name `net_listen`)\ncarrying ADR-0049 ruling 2's three clauses, ALL THREE:\n\n  1. absent from AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP and AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP -- mints no taint;\n  2. in _DISCLOSED_ONLY_BOUNDARIES with its own BoundaryMap count -- disclosed\n     without inflating the total_io_edges headline;\n  3. in OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES -- opacity-gating, so it WITHHOLDS `confirmed` on a\n     must_not_exist claim over the corresponding data boundary.\n\nCLAUSE 3 IS THE ONE THAT WILL BE OMITTED, AND OMITTING IT IS WORSE THAN SHIPPING\nNOTHING. Since INV-buzab, a call the catalogue CLASSIFIED is what `examined` means.\nA disclosure tag on ListenAndServe still classifies it -- so without clause 3 the\ncall counts as examined while minting nothing, and verify-claims returns a green\ntick over live ingress. That is not a hypothetical: ADR-0016's own table measures\nit in the outbound direction, where cataloguing `curl` as net_send took a\ncron-dropper claim from `inconclusive` rc 2 to `confirmed` rc 0. Six correct lines\nbought the false confirm.\n\nOPAQUE_BOUNDARIES ANTICIPATES THIS MEMBERSHIP, with one honest gap. Its governing\nsentence is \"boundaries whose classification records that the analysis CANNOT SEE\nPAST the call, rather than a known and complete I/O surface (INV-gahuz)\", and its\ndocstring says a future CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES member with that meaning \"belongs\nhere too, and the axis-conformance tests are what will ask\". But its EXAMPLE\nphrasing is \"control left this process\" -- the outbound instance. The inbound case\n(\"data will arrive at a scope this call does not name\") is covered by the general\nsentence, not the example. ADR-0049 makes that extension explicitly; update the\ndocstring so the next reader does not have to re-derive it.\n\nDO NOT REUSE command_launch. It is producer-stamped and DELIBERATELY disjoint from\nCATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES (a producer-stamped boundary is inert if it is in that\ntuple, because _parse_catalog iterates exactly it). Every affected row is\ncatalogue-declared. Take the policy, not the vehicle.\n\nREQUIRED EVIDENCE BEFORE MERGE:\n  * ADR-0016's three-row table reproduced for the new tag: absent / tagged /\n    tagged+opaque. If the middle row confirms a claim the first row withheld, the\n    design ships a false all-clear and must not merge.\n  * A represented-crossing proof per moved row (ADR-0049 ruling 3), RUN not\n    reasoned. `inconclusive` afterwards is a WI-lunav repeat, not a licence.\n  * ADR-0046 labelling: the gain accrues to USEFUL precision (KIND-MISDECLARED),\n    not correctness precision. Say which number moved.\n\nCOSTS, so they are not discovered late: schema-version bump (new BoundaryMap\nbucket, minor per io_boundary.py's own rules), verify-claims constraint surface,\nKNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES validation, ~49 references to the two boundary constants\nacross packages/ and scripts/, 100% coverage, shrink-only mypy ratchet, and a\nFULL test run because this is a data-file change. TestServerLaunchStaysAReceive\nmust be REWRITTEN, not deleted -- it is the pin, and its `accept` half must\nsurvive intact (accept is a transfer, not a family member).\n\nDECISIVENESS IS TRADED, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF DOING IT CAREFULLY: repos whose\ningress resolves to no arrival scope move from `violated` to\n`confirmed_with_caveats`. That is a real loss, taken deliberately in exchange for\nnot inventing the flow."  # 83f8
    fields: {}  # 83f8
- op: discuss  # 365e
  at: '2026-08-29T20:35:58Z'  # 365e
  by: agent  # 365e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 365e
  clock: 2  # 365e
  nonce: 365e  # 365e
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION FOUND BEFORE WRITING CODE. This item says clause 3 is 'in OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES'. That is WRONG and would ship an over-withholding gate.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED. The existing opacity machinery is BOUNDARY-AGNOSTIC. compute_boundary_coverage() takes no claim boundary; _opaque_launch_sites() returns sites; if any exist, BoundaryCoverage(complete=False) applies to EVERY claim regardless of which boundary it is about. That is correct for subprocess -- a launched program can do anything, so it makes every boundary unexaminable -- and wrong for a deferred-crossing tag. A listener tells you nothing about whether the program writes files. Adding net_listen to OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES as filed would send every Go server to inconclusive on fs_write, env_read and everything else.\n\nThat is the FALSE-ALL-CLEAR DIRECTION'S MIRROR: a change that reports LESS, on a gate that already withholds. It needs more evidence, not less, and it would have been easy to ship because the tests for subprocess opacity would all still pass.\n\nTHE ADR ALREADY DECIDED THIS, so it is not a new question. ADR-0049 ruling 2 clause 3 says the disclosure boundary 'withholds confirmed on a must_not_exist claim over the CORRESPONDING DATA BOUNDARY'. Scoped, not global. This item's one-line restatement dropped the scoping, and I wrote both.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS FOR SIZE. Clause 3 is not membership in an existing frozenset; it is NEW MACHINERY -- boundary-scoped opacity, which does not exist today. Shape:\n  * a map from disclosure boundary -> the data boundary it shadows (net_listen -> net_recv);\n  * BoundaryCoverage carries deferred sites KEYED BY the shadowed boundary;\n  * verify_claim consults it for the claim's own constraint.boundary at the three sites that read coverage.complete / qualifying_only / opaque_sites.\nThis touches the verdict path, which is the highest-risk code in the tool, so it lands with the ADR-0016 three-row table reproduced (absent / tagged / tagged+shadowed) as required evidence.\n\nSPLIT, so the risk is separable and no row moves before the census (WI-hazop):\n  PR 1 -- the boundary value, the disclosure count, and the scoped-opacity mechanism, with NO catalogue row using it yet. Fully testable against a synthetic row.\n  PR 2 -- the row moves, once WI-hazop's census says which rows and WI-vapud says they are reachable.\nRuling 3 is not violated by PR 1 because nothing is retagged in it."  # 365e
